\section{Introduction}
%\Algebraic topology is the study of spaces by means of topological invariants.
%Most prominent among topological invariants are homology \cite{analysissitus} and generalized homology theories whose development and formalization have a longstanding history  \cite{Steenrod},  \cite{Whitehead1962GeneralizedHT} and are one of the central topics of algebraic topology. 

%Homology is a creature that eats spaces and spits out graded rings. Although his teeth are sharp,
%homology is not dangerous. It is the spaces who are dangerous.
%
%It is goal of this paper to categorify homology in away that spaces are replaced by $\infty$-categories and graded rings are replaced by $\bE_1$-algebras in spectra of 
%$\infty$-categories for an appropriate categorified smash product.

The most prominent invariants of a pointed homotopy type $X$ are the homotopy groups
$\pi_*(X)$ and reduced homology groups $H_*(X),$ where we will omit the word ``reduced'' from now on.
The first are easier to define while the latter are easier to compute and are related to the first by the Hurewicz map $\pi_*(X) \to H_*(X) $, which is an isomorphism in degrees lower $n+2$ if $X$ is $n$-connected for $n >0$ by the Hurewicz theorem \cite{Hurewicz}. 
% that sends a homotopy class $S^n \to X$ to the induced map
%$H_n(S^n)=\bZ \to H_n(X)$ on homology, and in simplicial models is the canonical map
%$\pi_*(X)= \pi_*(\Sing(X)) \to \pi_*(\bZ[\Sing(X)]) = H_*(X) $, where $\Sing(X)$ is the singular complex.
If $X$ is connected, homology $H_*(X)$ is the homotopy  
of the infinite symmetric product $SP^\infty(X)$ by Dold-Thom's theorem \cite{Dold}.
In general, homology $H_*(X_+)$ is the chain homology of integral chains, which by Dold-Kan's theorem
identifies with the homotopy of the free simplicial abelian group
$\bZ[\Sing(X)]$ 
