Homotopy groups interact well with homotopy fiber sequences while homology groups interact well with homotopy cofiber sequences:
for every homotopy cofiber sequence of pointed homotopy types $A \to B \to C$ and integer $n \in \bZ $ there is an induced long exact sequence of abelian groups:
$$... \to H_{n +1}(A) \to H_{n +1}(B) \to H_{n +1}(C) \to H_n(A) \to H_n(B) \to H_n(C) \to H_{n-1}(A) \to H_{n-1}(B) \to ... $$

This property known as being homological explains the computational power of homology over homotopy since homotopy types are built by cells and attaching a cell defines a cofiber sequence.
Homology is unique with the property that the coefficients $H_*$ are $\bZ$,
a property known as dimension axiom, which was a motivation to study formally similar generalized homology theories like cobordism or topological $K$-theory that %do not satisfy the dimension axiom but 
have more general coefficients. The latter are useful to detect isomorphisms of homology theories:
by Whitehead's theorem a map of homology theories is an isomorphism if it induces an isomorphism on coefficients.
