A reduced homology theory is a functor $H: \mS_* \to Ab^\bZ$ from the $(\infty,1)$-category of pointed homotopy types to the category of graded abelian groups equipped with an isomorphism \begin{equation}\label{ii}
H \cong (H \circ \Sigma)[-1],
\end{equation}
where $\Sigma$ is the suspension and $[-1]$ is the shift,
that satisfies the Eilenberg-Steenrod axioms: the functor $H$ preserves filtered colimits, sends finite wedge sums to direct sums and is homological, i.e. sends cofiber sequences to exact sequences.
These axioms guarantees that 
%By parts of Brown's representability theorem 
any homology theory $H: \mS_* \to Ab^\bZ$ lifts to a functor $\bar{H}: \mS_* \to \mS_*$ along homotopy groups $\pi_*: \mS_* \to Ab^\bZ$.
The functor $\bar{H} : \mS_* \to \mS_*$ is reduced, i.e. preserves the contractible homotopy type, and therefore sends any pushout square of pointed homotopy types
\begin{equation}\label{ooo}
\begin{tikzcd}
X \ar{r}{} \ar{d}[swap]{} & 0 \ar{d}{} \\
0 \ar{r}[swap]{} & \Sigma(X)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
to a square of pointed homotopy types
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
\bar{H}(X) \ar{r}{} \ar{d}[swap]{} & 0 \ar{d}{} \\
0 \ar{r}[swap]{} & \bar{H}(\Sigma(X)).
\end{tikzcd}
\]
The latter square gives a map 
$
\bar{H}(X) \to \Omega(\bar{H}(\Sigma(X)))
$
by the universal property of the loop space, which induces on homotopy groups the isomorphism (\ref{ii}).
% \begin{equation}\label{excy}
%H(X)=\pi_* \bar{H}(X) \to \pi_* \Omega \bar{H} \Sigma(X) \cong \pi_* \bar{H}(\Sigma(X))[-1]= H(\Sigma(X))[-1].
%\end{equation} 
%and therefore is an equivalence.
In other words $\bar{H}$ sends pushout squares like (\ref{ooo}) to pullback squares. This property is known as excision and $\bar{H}$ is called an excisive functor. 
Conversely, for any reduced excisive functor $T: \mS_* \to \mS_*$ and any pointed homotopy type $X$ the graded set $\pi_*T(X)$ is isomorphic to the graded abelian group $ \pi_*\Omega^2(T(\Sigma^2(X)))=  \pi_{*+2}T	(\Sigma^2(X))$
and $\pi_* \circ T: \mS \to Ab^\bZ$ is a homology theory if $T$ preserves filtered colimits.
From this perspective a filtered colimits preserving reduced and excisive functor
$\mS_* \to \mS_*$ is a reasonable derived analogue of a homology theory,
%which is formally better behaved and also classified by a spectrum \cite[Theorem 1.4.2.24.]{lurie.HTT}.
which we call a derived homology theory for this reason.

