In analogy to the topological situation every categorical spectrum $E$
gives rise to an categorical homology theory $\infty\Cat_* \to \infty\Cat_* $
sending an $\infty$-category with distinguished object $X$ to 
$\Omega^\infty(E \wedge X),$ where $\Omega^\infty$ is the categorical analogue of the infinite loop space and $E \wedge X$ is the right tensor of a categorical spectrum with an $\infty$-category with distinguished object.
As shown in \cite[Proposition 13.4.14.]{HigherQuasi} every commutative monoid $M$ arises as  $\Omega^\infty$
of a unique categorical spectrum, the Eilenberg-MacLane categorical spectrum $H(M)$
of $M$. This is in complete analogy to the fact that every abelian group deloops to a spectrum, the Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum of the group.
In particular, we can study the categorical homology theory 
associated to the Eilenberg-MacLane categorical spectrum of any commutative monoid, which we call 
categorical $M$-homology. As an example of particular interest we can study categorical $\bN$-homology, which we call categorical homology.

It is goal of this article to demonstrate that the concept of categorical homology theory and
categorical homology
