The homology of a space $X$ is the homology of integral chains, which by Dold-Kan's theorem identifies with the homotopy of the free simplicial abelian group
$\bZ[\Sing(X)]$ on the singular complex.
In other words, since simplicial sets are a model for homotopy types and homology is a functor on the homotopy category, the homology of a homotopy type modeled by a simplicial set $X$ is the free simplicial abelian group $\bZ[X]$.
In analogy to simplicial abelian groups we consider stratified simplicial commutative monoids, i.e.
commutative monoid objects in the category of stratified simplicial sets, or more concretely, simplicial commutative monoids equipped with a stratification preserved by the operation.
The universal example is the 
