free stratified simplicial commutative monoid $\bN[X]$ on a stratified simplicial set $X$ that forms in each simplicial degree the free commutative monoid and whose distinguished $n$-simplices for $n >0$ are simply linear combinations of $n$-simplices with non-negative linear coefficients.
We prove the following theorem:
\begin{theorem}
The (non-reduced) categorical homology of an $\infty$-category modeled by a stratified simplicial set $\mC$ is the free stratified simplicial commutative monoid $\bN[\mC]$. % of the nerve of $\mC.$

\end{theorem}

Dold-Thom's theorem \cite{Dold} states that the reduced homology of a connected space $X$ is the homotopy of the infinite symmetric product $SP^\infty(X)$.
The infinite symmetric product $SP^\infty(X)$ is the colimit of the sequential diagram of strict symmetric powers 
$$ X \to X^{\times 2}_{\Sigma_2} \to X^{\times 3}_{\Sigma_3} \to ... $$
whose transition maps use the base point of $X$.
If $X$ is connected, by Dold-Thom the infinite symmetric product $SP^\infty(X)$ computes the grouplike strict commutative monoid on $X$,  which identifies with the free $H(\bZ)$-module spectrum. %in accordance with Dold-Thom. 
We prove an analogue for categorical homology.
We construct for any $\infty$-category with distinguished object $\mC$ a sequential diagram of strict symmetric powers
\begin{equation}\label{kl}
\mC \to \mC^{\times 2}_{\Sigma_2} \to \mC^{\times 3}_{\Sigma_3} \to ... \end{equation} 
whose transition maps use the distinguished object of $\mC$.

\begin{theorem}\label{klt}
The categorical homology of any $\infty$-category with distinguished object $\mC$ 
is the colimit of the sequential diagram \ref{kl}.

\end{theorem}

