Since categorical homology is represented by the Eilenberg-MacLane categorical spectrum $H\bN$, we prove the categorical Dold-Thom theorem (\cref{klt}) by identifying strict symmetric monoidal $\infty$-categories with connective left $\bH \bN$-module categorical spectra, where a categorical spectrum is connective if it arises as the infinite delooping of some symmetric monoidal $\infty$-category:
\begin{theorem}

There is a canonical equivalence
$$\C\mon(\infty\Cat) \simeq {_{H\bN}\Sp(\infty\Cat)_{\geq 0}} $$
between strict symmetric monoidal $\infty$-categories and connective left $\bH \bN$-module spectra.

\end{theorem}

We apply the categorified Dold-Thom theorem to prove a categorified Hurewicz theorem.
By the Hurewicz theorem for any connected homotopy type $X$ the Hurewicz map 
$\pi_1(X) \to H_1(X) $ exhibits $H_1(X)$ as the abelianization of $\pi_1(X)$
while for any $n >0$ and $n$-connected homotopy type $X$ the Hurewicz map 
$\pi_*(X) \to H_*(X) $ is an isomorphism in degrees lower $n+1$.
To categorify the situation in topology we call an $\infty$-category 
connected if its maximal subspace is connected.
More generally, for every natural $ n >0 $ we call an $\infty$-category 
$n$-connected if it is $0$-connected and all morphism $\infty$-categories are
$n-1$-connected.
For every $\infty$-category $\mC$ we define the set of weak equivalence classes in $\mC$
as the %set of equivalence classes in the classifying space of $\mC.$
quotient of the set of equivalence classes in $\mC$ under the equivalence relation of being connected by a zig-zag of morphisms in $\mC.$
We prove the following categorical Hurewicz theorem:

\begin{theorem}
Let $\mC $ be an $\infty$-category with distinguished object.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Then $H(\mC)$ is connected and the functor
$\Omega(\mC) \to \Omega(H(\mC)) $ induces on weak equivalence classes the universal map to the abelianization.
%exhibits the target as the abelianization of the monoid $\pi_0(\tau_0(\Omega(\mC))).$

\item Let $ n > 0$ and $\mC$ be $n$-connected. Then $H(\mC)$ is $n$-connected
and the functor $\Omega^{n+1}(\mC) \to \Omega^{n+1}(H(\mC))$ induces an isomorphism on 
weak equivalence classes.

