%$ Y \in  \Omega^{n+1}(\mC)$ whose image $Y' \in \Omega^{n+1}(\widetilde{\Sym}(\mC))$
%is connected to $Z$ by a zig-zag of morphisms.

\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}

As a second application of the categorified Dold-Thom theorem we compute the categorical homology of the globes, the walking higher cells:
For every natural $ n \geq 0$ there is a gaunt $(\infty,n)$-category classifying $n$-morphisms called the $n$-globe. The $0$-globe is the category with one object, the $1$-globe is the walking arrow
$\{0 < 1\}$, the 2-globe is the strict 2-category with two objects, two non-identity parallel morphisms and a unique non-identity 2-morphism connecting both parallel morphisms.
Like spheres are building blocks of homotopy types, the globes are building blocks of $\infty$-categories. Consequently, it seems crucial to understand categorical homology of the globes. We make the following computation:

\begin{theorem} ()
Let $n \geq 0.$ 

\begin{enumerate}

\item The non-reduced categorical homology of $\bD^k$ is the coproduct $$ \coprod_{n \geq0} (\bD^k)^{\vee n}.$$

\item The reduced categorical homology of $\bD^k$ is the sequential colimit of the diagram $$ \bD^0 \to \bD^k \to ... \to (\bD^k)^{\vee n} \to ... $$

\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}

For every commutative ring $R$ the functor of $R$-homology $ \mS_* \to Ab^\bZ$ 
carries additional structure coming from the fact that $R$ is a commutative ring and not merely an abelian group: it is a lax symmetric monoidal functor. This guarantees that $R$-homology $ \mS_* \to Ab^\bZ$ lifts to a lax symmetric monoidal functor $\mS_* \to \Mod_R(Ab^\bZ) \cong \Mod_R^\bZ$ to the category of graded $R$-modules.
The latter sends any $\bE_1$-space, for example loop space, to a graded algebra in $\Mod_R,$
and sends any $\bE_\infty$-space, for example infinite loop space, to a graded $R$-algebra.
We prove the categorical analogue of this situation:

\begin{theorem}\label{4} Let $R$ be a rig. 
Oplax $R$-homology $ \infty\Cat_* \to  \infty\Cat_*$ canonically lifts to a lax monoidal functor $$ \infty\Cat_* \to {_R\Mod_R(\Sp(\infty\Cat))}$$ to $R,R$-bimodule categorical spectra.

\end{theorem}

\cref{4} guarantees that categorical $R$-homology sends any monoidal $\infty$-category to an associative algebra in $ {_R\Mod_R(\Sp(\infty\Cat))}.$

As another example of categorical homology theory we consider categorical cobordism.
By \cite[Definition 1.4.6.]{Cob} there is an $\infty$-category of cobordisms whose objects are 0-manifolds and whose $n$-morphisms are $n$-fold cobordisms for $n \geq 0.$ Disjoint union endows the $\infty$-category of cobordisms with a symmetric monoidal structure whose infinite delooping gives a categorical spectrum which we name categorical cobordism spectrum. % whose classifying spectrum is the cobordism spectrum by the latter result.
The $\infty$-category of cobordisms exists in many flavors and variants
considering manifolds and cobordisms equipped with extra structure like framings, orientations or embeddings. % into a given manifold.
It is 
