\begin{example}\label{dimen}

%Let $n \geq 1$. An $\infty$-category is gaunt if and only if for every $n \geq 0$ the underlying $n$-category is gaunt.An $n$-category is gaunt if and only if the underlying 1-category and all morphism $n$-1-categories are gaunt.
An $\infty$-category is 1-strict if and only if the underlying category is an $(1,1)$-category.
An $\infty$-category is 1-gaunt if and only if the underlying category is an $(1,1)$-category
whose underlying groupoid is a set, i.e. every equivalence is an identity.

\end{example}

