\section{Categorical stability}

\subsection{Stable Gray-categories}

In this subsection we categorify the concept of stable $(\infty,1)$-category.

\begin{definition}
A right Gray-category $\mC$ is oplax quasi-stable if the following conditions are satisfied:

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mC$ admits a zero object.

\item $\mC$ admits endomorphisms.

\item The endomorphisms functor $\Omega: \mC \to \mC $ is an equivalence. 

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Any oplax quasi-stable right Gray-category $\mC$ also admits suspensions since $\Omega$ is an equivalence, where $\Sigma(X)\simeq \Omega^{-1}(X)$ for any $X \in \mC.$
Hence a right Gray-category $\mC$ is oplax quasi-stable if and only if the following dual conditions hold:

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mC$ admits a zero object.

\item $\mC$ admits suspensions.

\item The suspensions functor $\Sigma: \mC \to \mC $ is an equivalence. 

\end{enumerate}	

\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
A left Gray-category $\mC$ is lax quasi-stable if the right Gray-category $\mC^\co$ is oplax quasi-stable.

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A Gray-category is (op)lax quasi-stable if it is reduced and its underlying left (right) Gray-category is (op)lax quasi-stable.	

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A Gray-category is quasi-stable if it is lax and oplax quasi-stable.

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{agos}
A left (right) Gray-category $\mC$ is (op)lax quasi-stable if and only if the left (right) Gray-category $\mC^\op$ is (op)lax quasi-stable since $\Sigma_{\mC^\op} \simeq \Omega_\mC^\op.$
Consequently, a Gray-category $\mC$ is quasi-stable if and only if $\mC^\op$ is quasi-stable if and only if $\mC^\co$ is quasi-stable if and only if $\mC^{\co\op}$ is quasi-stable.

\end{remark}

