\subsection{Spectrum objects}

Next we define spectrum objects in any Gray-category. Spectra in the Gray-category $\infty\Cat_*$ were studied by Stefanich \cite[\S 13]{HigherQuasi}, \cite{Masuda} and \cite{kern2024categoricalspectrapointedinftymathbbzcategories} under the name categorical spectra.

\begin{definition}Let $\mC$ be a reduced Gray-category that admits endomorphisms.
The Gray-category $\Sp(\mC)$ of spectrum objects in $\mC$ is the sequential limit of the following  diagram of reduced Gray-categories: 
$$...\xrightarrow{\Omega} \mC^\cop \xrightarrow{\Omega} \mC  \xrightarrow{\Omega} \mC^\cop \xrightarrow{\Omega} \mC.$$

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}

Let $\mC$ be a reduced Gray-category that admits coendomorphisms.
The Gray-category of co-spectrum objects in $\mC$ is 
$$\Sp^\co(\mC):= \Sp(\mC^\co)^\co.$$
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{iwowo} %Let $\mC$ be a reduced Gray-category that admits coendomorphisms.
By definition there is a canonical equivalence $\Omega'_\mC \simeq \Omega^\co_{\mC^\co}$ of Gray-functors. Thus the Gray-category $\Sp^\co(\mC)$ is the sequential limit of the following  diagram of reduced Gray-categories:
$$...\xrightarrow{\Omega'} {^\cop\mC} \xrightarrow{\Omega'} \mC  \xrightarrow{\Omega'} {^\cop\mC} \xrightarrow{\Omega'} \mC.$$
\end{remark}

\begin{example}

The Gray-categories $\Sp(\mS), \Sp^\co(\mS)$ are the usual category of spectra viewed as a Gray-category.	

\end{example}

\begin{remark}\label{sptens}
Let $\mC$ be a presentable reduced Gray-category.
By \cref{reduc} the reduced Gray-functor $\Sigma: \mC \to \mC$ factors as
$\mC \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \Sigma: \mC \simeq \mC \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \infty\Cat_* \to \mC \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \infty\Cat_* \simeq \mC$ and $\Sigma': \mC \to \mC$ factors as
$\Sigma' \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \mC : \mC \simeq \infty\Cat_* \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \mC \to \infty\Cat_* \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \mC\simeq \mC.$
Hence there are canonical equivalences of reduced Gray-categories $\Sp(\mC) \simeq \mC \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \Sp$ and $\Sp^\co(\mC)\simeq \Sp^\co  \ot_{\infty\Cat_*} \mC.$ 

%In particular, $\Sp(\mC)$ is 

\end{remark}

By \cref{reduc} there is a canonical equivalence of Gray-functors $ \Omega^2 \simeq \Omega'^2: \infty\Cat_* \to \infty\Cat_*.$ Consequently, there is a canonical equivalence of Gray-categories $$ \Sp(\infty\Cat_*) \simeq \lim(... \xrightarrow{\Omega^2} \mC \xrightarrow{\Omega^2}\mC)  \simeq \lim(... \xrightarrow{\Omega'^2} \mC \xrightarrow{\Omega'^2}\mC) \simeq \Sp^\co(\infty\Cat_*),$$
where the first and third equivalence are by cofinality.

\begin{definition}
The Gray-category of categorical spectra is $$\Sp := \Sp(\infty\Cat_*) \simeq \Sp^\co(\infty\Cat_*).$$	
\end{definition}

\begin{notation}Let $\mC$ be a reduced Gray-category that admits endomorphisms.
The reduced Gray-functor of infinite endomorphisms $$\Omega^\infty: \Sp(\mC) \to \mC$$ is the canonical Gray-functor projecting to the value of the final object of the diagram.

\end{notation}

\begin{remark}\label{nnm}
If $\mC$ is a reduced presentable Gray-category, the Gray-functor $\Omega$ admits a left adjoint and so the Gray-category $\Sp(\mC)$ is presentable:
the subcategory $\wedge\Pr^R\wedge $ of presentable Gray-categories and right adjoint Gray-functors admits small limits preserved by the inclusion $\wedge\Pr^R\wedge \subset {\wedge\widehat{\Cat}\wedge}.$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{nnmt}
Via the equivalence $\wedge\Pr^L\wedge \simeq (\wedge\Pr^R\wedge)^\op$ and \cref{nnm} the Gray-category $\Sp(\mC)$ is also the colimit in $\wedge\Pr^L\wedge$ of the sequential diagram $$\mC \xrightarrow{\Sigma} \mC^\cop \xrightarrow{\Sigma} \mC \xrightarrow{\Sigma}....$$
\end{remark}

%For the next lemma observe that for every compactly generated Gray-category$\mC$ the full subcategories $ (\mC_*)^\omega, (\mC^\omega)_*$ of $\mC_*$ coincide and we write $\mC^\omega_*$ for both.

The following are two important examples of categorical spectra, which appear in
\cite[Example 13.3.10., Example 13.3.12.]{HigherQuasi}:

\begin{example} 
By \cite[Definition 5.16.]{HaugsengSpans}	for every $n \geq 0$ and category $\mC$ that admits finite limits there is an $n$-category $\mathrm{Span}^n(\mC)$ of $n$-fold spans in $\mC$ and a functor $ \mathrm{Span}^n(\mC) \to \mathrm{Span}^{n+1}(\mC)$ that induces an equivalence on maximal subspaces such that $\mathrm{Span}^0(\mC) \simeq \mC$. 
%and $\mathrm{Span}(\mC):=\mathrm{Span}^1(\mC)$ is the 2-category of spans in $\mC$. 
By \cite[Proposition 8.3.]{HaugsengSpans} for every $X,Y \in \mC$ there is an equivalence
$$ \Mor_{ \mathrm{Span}^{n+1}(\mC)}(X,Y) \simeq \mathrm{Span}^{n}(\mC_{/ X \times Y}).$$
In particular, there is an equivalence $ \nu_n: \Mor_{ \mathrm{Span}^{n+1}(\mC)}(\ast,\ast) \simeq  \mathrm{Span}^{n}(\mC).$
Consequently, the sequence $$\mathrm{corr}(\mC):= \{ \mathrm{Span}^{n}(\mC),  \nu_n \} $$ is a categorical spectrum.

\end{example}

\begin{example}

By \cite[Theorem 3.4.4.2.]{lurie.higheralgebra} for every small symmetric monoidal category $\mC$ the free functor $\infty\Cat \to \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$, where modules are taken with respect to the product, is symmetric monoidal and so exhibits $ \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$ as a presentable $\infty\Cat$-enriched category, i.e. presentable $\infty$-category.
The $\infty$-category of endomorphisms of $\mC$ in $ \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$
is the initial commutative algebra in $ \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$, which is $\mC.$ 
In other words $  \Mor_{\Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)}(\mC,\mC) \simeq \mC.$

If $\mC$ admits left tensors preserved by the tensor product component-wise,
by \cite[Theorem 7.21.]{HEINE2023108941} the functor
$ \Mod_{\mC}(\infty\Cat) \to \Mod_{\iota_1\mC}(\Cat)$ induced by $\iota_1$ 
restricts to an equivalence between the full subcategories of $\infty$-categories with right $\mC$-action having left tensors preserved by the right $\mC$-action component-wise, 
and the categories with right $\iota_1(\mC)$-action whose restriction to $\infty\Cat $ exhibits the category as right $\infty\Cat$-enriched.

By \cite[Proposition 4.3.2.5.]{lurie.higheralgebra} for every associative algebra $A$ in $\iota_1(\mC)$ the category $_{A}\Mod(\iota_1(\mC))$ is right tensored over $\iota_1(\mC)$ and via restriction to $\infty\Cat $ right $\infty\Cat$-enriched. Hence it uniquely lifts to an object of $\Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$ denoted by $ _{A}\Mod(\mC). $  
Let $\mathrm{Morita}(\mC) \subset  \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$
be the full subcategory spanned by ${_A\Mod(\mC)}$ for $A $ an associative algebra in $\mC$.
If $\mC$ admits $\kappa$-small colimits preserved by the tensor product component-wise for some small regular cardinal $\kappa, $ by \cref{small} the $\infty$-category $\mathrm{Morita}(\mC)$ is small.

Setting $$  \mathrm{Morita}^{n+1}(\mC):=   \mathrm{Morita}(\mathrm{Morita}^n(\mC))$$
for every $n \geq 0$ the canonical equivalence $ \Mor_{\mathrm{Morita}(\mC)}(\mC,\mC) \simeq \Mor_{\Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)}(\mC,\mC) \simeq \mC$ provides an equivalence
$  \nu_n: \mathrm{Morita}^n(\mC) \simeq \Mor_{\mathrm{Morita}^{n+1}(\mC)}( \mathrm{Morita}^n(\mC), \mathrm{Morita}^n(\mC)) .$ 
Consequently, the sequence 
$$\mathrm{morita}(\mC)  := \{ \mathrm{Morita}^n(\mC), \nu_n \}$$ is a categorical spectrum. 

\end{example}

\begin{definition}\label{Morita} Let $R$ be an $\bE_\infty$-ring spectrum.
The Morita (categorical) spectrum of $R$ is $\mathrm{morita}(\Mod_R(\Sp(\mS))).$

\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{small} Let $\kappa$ be a small regular cardinal and $\mC$ a small symmetric monoidal $\infty$-category such that $\mC$ is small and admits $\kappa$-small colimits and left tensors preserved by the tensor product component-wise. The $\infty$-category $\mathrm{Morita}(\mC)$ is small.

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

The $\infty$-categories $\infty\Cat$ and so also $\Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat)$ are locally small, i.e. the morphism $\infty$-categories are small. We prove that the maximal subspace in $\mathrm{Morita}(\mC)$ is small.
Let $\Cat(\kappa) \subset \Cat$ be the subcategory of small $\infty$-categories having $\kappa$-small colimits and functors preserving $\kappa$-small colimits.
By \cite[Corollary 4.8.1.4.]{lurie.higheralgebra} there is a closed symmetric monoidal structure on $\Cat(\kappa)$ such that the inclusion $\Cat(\kappa) \subset \Cat$ is lax symmetric monoidal. The latter gives rise to an inclusion
$ \Mod_{\iota_1\mC}(\Cat(\kappa)) \subset \Mod_{\iota_1\mC}(\Cat) \to \Mod_{\mC}(\infty\Cat).$
The assumptions imply that $\Mod_A(\mC)$ belongs to  $\Mod_{\iota_1\mC}(\Cat(\kappa))$.
%If $\mC$ admits $\kappa$-small colimits and the tensor product of $\mC$ is component-wise $\infty\Cat$-linear and $\kappa$-small colimits preserving, then also $_A\Mod(\mC)$ admits $\kappa$-small colimits and the right $\mC$-action is component-wise $\infty\Cat$-linear and $\kappa$-small colimits preserving. Consequently, $_A\Mod(\mC)$ belongs to the subcategory$\Mod_{\iota_1(\mC)}(\Cat(\kappa)) \subset \Mod_\mC(\infty\Cat). $
The inclusion $\Cat(\kappa) \subset \Cat$ preserves small $\kappa$-filtered colimits because
$\kappa$-small categories are $\kappa$-compact in $\Cat$ by \cite[Proposition 5.4.1.2.]{lurie.HTT}.
This implies that the functor $\Mod_{\iota_1\mC}(\Cat(\kappa)) \to \mS, \mM \mapsto \iota_0({_A\Mod}(\mM))$ preserves small $\kappa$-filtered colimits. 
By \cite[Theorem 5.1.]{HEINE2023108941} the latter functor is represented by $_A\Mod(\iota_1(\mC))$, which therefore is a $\kappa$-compact object of $ \Mod_{\iota_1(\mC)}(\Cat(\kappa))$. Thus the maximal subspace of $\mathrm{Morita}(\mC)$ is contained in the maximal subspace of the full subcategory $\Mod_{\iota_1(\mC)}(\Cat(\kappa)) $ spanned by the $\kappa$-compact objects, which is small.

\end{proof}

\begin{definition}\label{Brau} Let $R$ be an $\bE_\infty$-ring spectrum.
The Morita spectrum $\mathrm{morita}(R) $ of $R$ is the Morita spectrum of $\Mod_R.$	

\end{definition}

Next we prove compact generation of the Gray-category of categorical spectra and more generally spectrum objects.

