\begin{theorem}\label{Brown}
Let $\mD$ be a presentable reduced Gray-category such that endomorphisms commute with sequential colimits.
The left Gray-functor $$\nu: {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$$
refines to a Gray-functor that
%there is a Gray-functor $${\Fil\Lax\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$$ 
%that induces $\nu$ on underlying left Gray-categories and 
%sends $\F$ to $\{\F(S^n), \F(S^n) \to \Omega^2\F(S^{n+2})\}$
%and that 
admits a fully faithful left adjoint sending $\E \in \Sp(\mD)$ to $ \Omega^\infty(\E \wedge (-)).$
The left adjoint induces an equivalence of Gray-categories $\Sp(\mD) \simeq \mH(\mD) \subset  {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD).$
%\item A right Gray-functor $ \mathrm{Sph} \to \infty\Cat$ belongs to the essential image of $\N$ if and only if it is a lax homology theory.
%for every even $n \geq 0$the canonical functor $\F(S^n)\to \Omega^2\F(S^{n+2})$ is an equivalence.\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof} 

By \cite[Proposition 5.7.]{heine2024higher} evaluation at $S^0$ induces an equivalence of left Gray-categories
$${\L\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\Sp(\mD)) \to \Sp(\mD),$$
whose inverse sends $\E$ to $\E \wedge (-).$
The Gray-functor $\Omega^\infty:\Sp(\mD) \to \mD$ induces a left Gray-functor
$$ {\Fil\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\Sp(\mD)) \to {\Fil\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD).$$
We obtain a left Gray-functor $$\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \simeq {\L\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\Sp(\mD)) \subset {\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\Sp(\mD))\to {\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$$
that sends $\E$ to $\Omega^\infty(\E \wedge(-))$ and so by \cref{homol} lands in $\mH(\mD) \subset {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD).$

We prove next that $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$
preserves small colimits and left tensors. By \cref{rioko} the embedding $$\mH(\mD) \subset {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$$ preserves small colimits and left tensors.
So it is enough to see that $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to \mH(\mD)$ preserves small colimits and left tensors.
The left Gray-functor $$\nu: {\Fil\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to \Pre\Sp(\mD)$$ preserves small colimits and left tensors, preserves local objects and by \cref{locpre} also preserves local equivalences.
This implies that the restriction $$\nu: {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$$ 
preserves small colimits and left tensors.
By \cref{rioko} the embedding $$\mH(\mD) \subset {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$$ preserves small colimits and left tensors and $\mH(\mD)$ admits small colimits and left tensors.
So the restriction $\nu: \mH(\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$ is a left Gray-functor that preserves small colimits and left tensors between left Gray-categories that admit small colimits and left tensors.
By the definition of lax homology theory $\nu: \mH(\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$ is conservative and so also detects 
small colimits and left tensors. Consequently, $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to \mH(\mD)$ preserves small colimits
and left tensors if and only if the composition $\nu \circ \Psi$ does.
There is a canonical equivalence $$ \nu \circ \Psi \simeq \Omega^\infty((-)\wedge S^\bullet) \simeq (-) \simeq S^0 \simeq \id$$ of left Gray-functors $\Sp(\mD) \to \Sp(\mD)$.
Hence $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to \mH(\mD)$ and so also $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to \mH(\mD) \subset  {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) $ preserve small colimits and left tensors.

We prove next that the equivalence $\lambda: \id \simeq \nu \circ \Phi$ exhibits $\Phi$ as left adjoint to $\nu.$
Since $\lambda$ is an equivalence, this will imply that $\Phi$ is fully faithful.
So we verify that for every $\E \in \Sp(\mD)$ and $H \in  {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ the induced functor
$$\theta: \L\Mor_{{\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)}(\Psi(\E), H) \to \L\Mor_{\Sp(\mD)} (\E,\nu(H)) $$ is an equivalence.
By \cref{spgen} the left Gray-category $\Sp(\mD) $ is generated under small colimits by the images of $\Omega^n(S)$ for even $n \geq 0$ under the left Gray-functors $(Y \wedge (-))_!: \Sp \to \Sp(\mD)$
induced by the left adjoint left Gray-functors $Y \wedge (-): \infty\Cat_* \to \mD$ for every $Y \in \mD$.
Since $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) $ preserves small colimits, %and left tensors
we can assume that $\E= (Y \wedge (-))_!(\Omega^n(S))$ for $n \geq 0$ even and $Y \in \mD.$
In this case the functor  $\theta$ factors as
$$ \L\Mor_{{\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)}(\Psi(\E), H) \to $$$$ \L\Mor_{{\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)}((Y \wedge (-)) \circ \Omega^n, H) \simeq $$
$$\L\Mor_{{\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\infty\Cat_*)}(\Omega^n, \R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ H)\simeq $$$$
\L\Mor_{{\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\infty\Cat_*)}(\L\Mor_{\infty\Cat_*}(S^n,-), \R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ H)
\simeq$$$$ \R\Mor_\mD(Y,H(S^n)) \simeq \nu(\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ H)_n $$$$ \simeq 
\Omega^\infty(\Sigma^n(\nu(\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ H))) \simeq $$
$$\L\Mor_{\Sp}(S, \Sigma^n(\nu(\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-)\circ H)))  \simeq$$$$
\L\Mor_{\Sp}(\Omega^n(S), \nu(\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ H)) \simeq $$$$ \L\Mor_{\Sp} (\Omega^n(S),\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-) \circ \nu(H)) $$$$ \simeq \L\Mor_{\Sp(\mD)} (\E,\nu(H)).$$ 

Consequently, we need to see that the canonical morphism $$ (Y \wedge (-)) \circ \Omega^n \to \Psi(\E)= \Omega^\infty(  (Y \wedge (-))_!(\Omega^n(S)) \wedge(-) )  \simeq \Omega^\infty( \Omega^n( (Y \wedge (-))_!(S)) \wedge(-) )  \simeq $$$$  \Omega^n(\Omega^\infty(\Sigma^\infty(Y) \wedge(-) ))  \simeq  \Omega^n \circ  \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )  $$ exhibits the target as oplax excisive approximation.
By \cref{appro} the canonical morphism $$ \Omega^n \to \Omega^n \circ \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty %\simeq \Omega^\infty(\Omega^n(S) \wedge (-))
$$ exhibits the target as oplax excisive approximation.
The reduced left Gray-functor $$(Y \wedge (-))_!: {\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\infty\Cat_*) \to {\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$$ preserves local equivalences since its right adjoint
$$\R\Mor_\mD(Y,-)_!: {\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to {\Fil\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\infty\Cat_*)$$ preserves local objects. Hence the canonical morphism \begin{equation}\label{lal}
 (Y \wedge (-)) \circ \Omega^n \to (Y \wedge (-)) \circ  \Omega^n \circ \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \end{equation} is a local equivalence. 
So we need to see that the canonical morphism
$$ (Y \wedge (-)) \circ  \Omega^n \circ \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty  \to \Omega^n \circ  \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )$$ 
exhibits the target as oplax excisive approximation, or equivalently that
the induced morphism $$ (Y \wedge (-)) _! ( \Omega^n \circ \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \to \Omega^n \circ  \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )$$  
is an equivalence.
The latter factors as $$ (Y \wedge (-)) _! ( \Omega^n \circ \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \simeq \Omega^n \circ (Y \wedge (-)) _! (\Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \to \Omega^n \circ  \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) ), $$ 
where the first equivalence is by \cref{excend}.
So it suffices to show that the canonical morphism $$ (Y \wedge (-)) _! (\Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \to \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )$$ is an equivalence.
The latter is equivalent to say that the canonical morphism $$ (Y \wedge (-)) \circ (\Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \to \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )$$ is a local equivalence.
Since the canonical morphism $$(Y \wedge (-)) \to (Y \wedge (-)) \circ (\Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty) $$ of \ref{lal} for $n=0$ is a local equivalence, it is enough to see that the composition
$$(Y \wedge (-)) \to (Y \wedge (-)) \circ (\Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty)  \to \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^\infty \circ (Y \wedge(-) )$$ is a local equivalence.
This follows from \cref{appro}.

Thus we obtain a left Gray-adjunction \begin{equation}\label{lokyz} 
\Psi : \Sp(\mD) \rightleftarrows {\Fil\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) :\nu,\end{equation} where $\Psi$ is an embedding that lands in $\mH(\mD).$
Hence the latter adjunction restricts to a left Gray-adjunction $$ \Psi : \Sp(\mD) \rightleftarrows \mH(\mD) :\nu,$$ where the left adjoint is an embedding and the right adjoint is conservative.
Consequently, $\Psi: \Sp(\mD) \to \mH(\mD)$ is an equivalence inverse to
$\nu: \mH(\mD) \to \Sp(\mD).$ In particular, the underlying left Gray-functor of the embedding $\mH(\mD) \subset \Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ of Gray-categories admits a right adjoint.

We complete the proof by enhancing the left Gray-adjunction \ref{lokyz} to a Gray-adjunction.

The full subcategory $\mH(\mD)$ is closed in ${\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ under right tensors: for every $Y \in \infty\Cat_*$ and spectrum $\E$
there is a canonical equivalence $$ \Omega^\infty(\E\wedge(-)) \wedge Y = \Omega^\infty(\E\wedge(-)) \circ (Y \wedge(-)) \simeq \Omega^\infty((\E\wedge Y) \wedge(-)).$$

By \cref{cloten} the full subcategories $$ \Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD), {\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$$
are closed in ${\Fun\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) $ under right tensors. 
Hence $\mH(\mD)$ is also closed under right tensors in $\Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ and ${\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$. Since the underlying left Gray-functor of the embedding $\mH(\mD) \subset \Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ of Gray-categories admits a right adjoint, the first statement implies by \cref{adj}
that the embedding $\mH(\mD) \subset \Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD)$ of Gray-categories admits a right adjoint $$R: \Fil{\Oplax\Exc\wedge}(\infty\Cat_*,\mD) \to \mH(\mD).$$

