\begin{lemma}\label{realprod}

The realization $|-|: \s\Set^{t\Delta^\op}\cong \Fun(\Delta^\op, \Set^{t\Delta^\op}) \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ preserves finite products.	

\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}

%the functor $(-)^{\#}: \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op} $ preserves small colimits and small limits. % because small colimits and small limits in $ \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ are preserves and reflected by the forgetful functor$  \Set^{t\Delta^\op} \to \s\Set \times \Set^\bN, (X,\mE) \mapsto , (X,\mE)$ and the composition$ (-)^{\#}: \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op} \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op} \to \s\Set \times \Set$ sends $X$ to $ (X, (X_n)_{n \in \Delta} ).$

Let $\iota: \Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ be the diagonal embedding, which preserves small colimits and small limits and $\iota_*:  \s\Set= \Fun(\Delta^\op, \Set) \to \Fun(\Delta^\op, \Set^{t\Delta^\op})\cong \s\Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ the induced functor. 
By uniqueness of Yoneda-extension the left adjoint functor $|-| \circ \iota_*:  \sSet \to \s\Set^{t\Delta^\op}\cong \Fun(\Delta^\op, \Set^{t\Delta^\op}) \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ and the left adjoint functor $(-)^{\#}: \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ of \cref{exammp} are equivalent since they are equivalent after restriction along the Yoneda-embedding.
%sends $\iota_* (\Delta^n) $ to $(\Delta^n)^{\#}.$ 
%Hence the composition $|-| \circ \iota_*:  \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ of small colimits preserving functors sends $\Delta^n $ to $(\Delta^n)^{\#}$ and therefore by uniqueness of Yoneda-extension is equivalent to the functor$(-)^{\#}: \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ that preserves small colimits by \cref{exammp}.
By \cref{exammp} the functor $(-)^{\#}: \s\Set \to \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ preserves small limits and so finite products. Consequently, for every $n, \m \geq 0$ the canonical map $$|\iota_*(\Delta^n) \times \iota_*(\Delta^\m) | \cong |\iota_*(\Delta^n \times \Delta^\m) |  \to |\iota_*(\Delta^n)|  \times |\iota_*(\Delta^\m)| $$ is an isomorphism.
Thus for every $X, Y \in \Set^{t\Delta^\op}$ the canonical map $$|\iota_*(\Delta^n) \times X \times \iota_*(\Delta^\m) \times Y |   \cong |\iota_*(\Delta^n) \times \iota_*(\Delta^\m) \times X \times Y | 
\cong |\iota_*(\Delta^n) \times \iota_*(\Delta^\m)| \times X \times Y 
\to $$$$ | \iota_*(\Delta^n) | \times | \iota_*(\Delta^\m) | \times X \times Y \cong| \iota_*(\Delta^n) | \times X  \times | \iota_*(\Delta^\m) | \times Y \cong | \iota_*(\Delta^n) \times X |  \times | \iota_*(\Delta^\m) \times Y | $$ is an isomorphism.
This implies the result since $ \Set^{t\Delta^\op}, \s\Set^{t \Delta^\op} $ are cartesian closed being categories of presheaves and $\s\Set^{t \Delta^\op} \cong \Fun(\Delta^\op, \Set^{t \Delta^\op})$ is a generated under small colimit by objects of the form $\iota_*(\Delta^n) \times X$ for $n \geq 0$ and $X \in  \Set^{t\Delta^\op}.$

\end{proof}

