\begin{theorem}\label{Grayadj}
Let $0 \leq n \leq \infty$ and $\bk \geq n$ and $\mB$ an $n$-category and $\mC$ an $\infty$-category that admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk$. The $\infty$-categories $ \Fun^\lax(\mB,\mC), \Fun^\oplax(\mB,\mC) $ admit left (right) adjoints
for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-n$. 

\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \cref{grayhoms} there is an equivalence $ \Fun^\oplax(\mB,\mC) \simeq \Fun^\lax(\mB^\op,\mC^\op)^\op. $ So it suffices to prove the statement for $ \Fun^\lax(\mB,\mC).$

Since $n\Cat$ is generated under small colimits by the disks of dimension smaller $n+1$ and the full subcategory of $\infty\Cat$ spanned by the small $\infty$-categories that admit left (right) adjoints 
for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-n$ is closed under small limits and the functor $\Fun^\lax(-,\mC): n\Cat^\op \to \infty\Cat$ preserves limits, we can assume that $\mB=\bD^\ell$ for $0 \leq \ell \leq n.$
So it suffices to show that for every $0 \leq n \leq \infty$ and $\bk \geq n$ and $\infty$-category $\mC$ that admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk$ the $\infty$-category $ \Fun^\lax(\bD^n,\mC) $ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-n$. 

We proceed by induction on $n \geq 0.$ The case $n=0$ is trivial since $\Fun^\lax(\bD^0,\mC) \simeq \mC.$ Assume the statement for $n$ holds.
%at $ \Fun^\lax(\bD^n,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints. % for every $\infty$-category $\mC$. 
By \cref{thas2} there is a canonical equivalence $\bD^{n+1} \simeq \bD^n \boxtimes \bD^1 \coprod_{\bD^n \boxtimes \partial\bD^1} \partial\bD^1$
that induces an equivalence 
$$\Fun^\lax(\bD^{n+1},\mC) \simeq \mC \times \mC \times_{\Fun^\lax(\bD^{n},\mC) \times \Fun^\lax(\bD^{n},\mC)} \Fun^\lax(\bD^{1},\Fun^\lax(\bD^{n},\mC)).$$
Since the full subcategory of $\infty\Cat$ of small $\infty$-categories having left (right) adjoints
for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-n$ is closed under small limits, by induction on $n \geq 0$ it is enough to show the statement for $n=1,$ i.e. $\mB=\bD^1.$

To complete the proof we prove by induction on $\bk \geq 3$ that for every $\infty$-category $\mC$ 
having left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk$ the $\infty$-category  
$\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-1.$
The case $\bk=3$ is \cref{adjjo}. We assume that the statement holds for $\bk.$
%that for every $\infty$-category $\mC$ having left (right) adjoints the $\infty$-category $ \Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk$.
Let $\mC$ be an $\infty$-category having left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk+1.$
We need to see that $ \Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk$. 
By assumption $ \Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-1$. So it remains to see that for every morphisms $f:A \to B, g: X \to Y$ in $\mC$ corresponding to objects of $\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ the morphism $\infty$-category $\Mor_{\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)}(f,g)$ admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-1.$
By \cref{homs} there is an equivalence
$$\Mor_{\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)}(f,g) \simeq \Mor_{\mC}(A,X) \times \Mor_{\mC}(B,Y) \times_{\Mor_{\mC}(A,Y) \times \Mor_{\mC}(A,Y)} \Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\Mor_{\mC}(A,Y)).$$
By induction hypothesis the latter $\infty$-category admits left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-1$ because the full subcategory of $\infty\Cat$ spanned by the small $\infty$-categories having left (right) adjoints for morphisms of dimension smaller $\bk-1$ is closed under small limits.
%It remains to prove the induction start, where $\bk=2.$ The statement for $\bk=2$ says that $\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right) adjoints for 1-morphisms or equivalently, that the underlying 2-category of $\Fun^\lax(\bD^1,\mC)$ admits left (right adjoints). This holds by \cref{adjjo}.
%We first prove the case of right adjoints. 

\end{proof}

