\begin{abstract}

Documenting tacit knowledge in organizations can be a challenging task due to incomplete initial information, difficulty in identifying knowledgeable individuals, the interplay of formal hierarchies and informal networks, and the need to ask the right questions. To address this, we propose an agent-based framework leveraging large language models (LLMs) to iteratively reconstruct dataset descriptions through interactions with employees. Modeling knowledge dissemination as a Susceptible-Infectious (SI) process with waning infectivity, we conduct 864 simulations across various synthetic company structures and different dissemination parameters. Our results show that the agent achieves 94.9\% full-knowledge recall, with self-critical feedback scores strongly correlating with external literature critic scores. We analyze how each simulation parameter affects the knowledge retrieval process for the agent. In particular, we find that our approach is able to recover information without needing to access directly the only domain specialist. These findings highlight the agent’s ability to navigate organizational complexity and capture fragmented knowledge that would otherwise remain inaccessible.

%Documenting tacit knowledge within organizations presents significant challenges due to incomplete initial information and the dynamic interplay of formal hierarchies and informal networks. To address this, we propose an agent-based framework leveraging large language models (LLMs) to iteratively reconstruct dataset descriptions through interactions with employees. We perform a series of simulations, grounded on social theory concepts, in which we consider the knowledge about a hypothetical table tacit, and the knowledge dissemination is modeled as an epidemic Susceptible-Infectious (SI) framework with waning infectivity where facts propagate unevenly across the synthetic company structures. Over 864 simulations, our experiments evaluate different parameter combinations for company structure and knowledge dissemination. Results demonstrate the agent’s ability to achieve 94.9\% full-knowledge recall across simulations, with reference-free self-critical feedback scores showing strong correlation to other literature critic scores with and without added reference. Our analysis summarize how each simulation parameter affects the knowledge retrieval process for the agent. In particular, we find that our approach is able to recover information without needing to access directly the only domain specialist. These findings highlight the capacity of our approach to navigate the organizational complexity and collect fragmented information, documenting the tacit knowledge that could otherwise be inaccessible.


%Documenting tacit knowledge within organizations presents significant challenges due to incomplete initial information and the dynamic interplay of formal hierarchies and informal networks. To address this, we propose an agent-based framework leveraging large language models (LLMs) to iteratively reconstruct dataset descriptions through interactions with employees. Organizational knowledge dissemination is modeled using an epidemic Susceptible-Infectious (SI) framework with waning infectivity, where facts propagate unevenly across synthetic company structures. Experiments systematically evaluate different parameter combinations for company structure and knowledge dissemination. Results demonstrate the agent’s ability to achieve 94.9\% full-knowledge recall across simulations, with reference-free self-critical feedback scores show strong correlation to the same critic with added reference. Our analysis of the experiments shows how each simulation parameter affects the knowledge retrieval process for the agent, and we find that our approach is able to recover the original information without needing to access the only domain specialist. These findings highlight the capacity of the framework to navigate organizational complexity while exposing tensions between structural parameters, knowledge distribution patterns, and automated evaluation methodologies.

\end{abstract}

