\appendix
\section{Computational Expense Considerations}\label{app:comp_cost}
Optimizing parameters in chaotic systems—especially using orders like orders—requires numerous simulations due to sensitivity to changes. Our finite-difference simulation requires two evaluations per parameter per iteration. Let:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \( d \): Number of parameters (e.g., \( T_{\text{con}}, K, R_v \)),
    \item \( E \): Number of SGD iterations,
    \item \( T \): Time steps in each simulation,
    \item \( \Delta u \): Integration step size.
\end{itemize}

Each gradient requires two simulations per parameter, so total simulations are:
\begin{equation}
N_{\text{sim}} = 2d \cdot E.
\end{equation}
With complexity:
\[
\mathcal{O}(2dET) = \mathcal{O}(dET).
\]
For \( d=3 \), \( E=100 \), \( T=284 \):
\[
N_{\text{sim}} = 600, \quad \mathcal{O}(1.7 \times 10^5).
\]
Adding two parameters (\( d=5 \)):
\[
N_{\text{sim}} = 1000, \quad \mathcal{O}(2.84 \times 10^5),
\]
a 67\% increase. Expanding the cost function with more terms raises costs linearly, necessitating careful justification for added complexity.

