\section{Conclusion}

In this work, we propose \textit{Divergence Minimization Preference Optimization (\method)}, a novel and theoretically grounded framework for aligning diffusion models with human preferences. Unlike prior methods that rely on forward KL divergence or implicit reward modeling, \method leverages reverse KL divergence to achieve mode-seeking behavior, enabling more precise and robust preference alignment. We provide a rigorous derivation of \method under the diffusion framework, including the formulation of alignment intervals. Through comprehensive experiments across multiple datasets and reward models, we demonstrate that \method consistently outperforms existing alignment baselines, showing stronger adherence to user preference. \method offers a principled and effective solution to the challenge of preference alignment in diffusion models, bridging theoretical insights and practical performance. We hope this work inspires further research into divergence-based alignment objectives for text-to-image generative models.

