\begin{abstract}
Recently, moiré superlattices have been found on the surface of topological insulators due to the rotational misalignment of topmost layers. 
In this work, we study the effects of moiré superlattices on the Landau levels of topological surface states. 
We find that an extra Zeeman term besides the intrinsic one can be induced by the orbital effect of the magnetic field in moir\'e surface states. 
As a result, the originally field-independent zeroth Landau level of moir\'e surface states could be tilted by the out-of-plane magnetic field.
%The Moiré superlattice has attracted widespread attention as a result of its tunable properties and rich many-body effects. 
%A moir\'e superlattice on the surface of a topological insulator is different from the moir\'e superlattice on the trivial-topological substrate, which generates a series of satellite Dirac cones. 
%In this paper, we theoretically investigate the properties of the surface Dirac cone from a moir\'e superlattice on the surface of a topological insulator under an out-of-plane magnetic field. We find that the lowest-energy Dirac cone is robust under a magnetic field because of the topological nature of the bulk, while the additional satellite Dirac cone will be gapped out.  
\end{abstract}
