\textit{\textcolor{blue}{Introduction.}}---
It is said that an external magnetic field has two effects on an electron, the orbital effect that couples to momentum, and the Zeeman effect that couples to spin. The orbital effect can be inherited from classical electrodynamics, while the origin of the Zeeman effect was unknown until 1928, when Paul Dirac conceived his famous relativistic equation for electrons \cite{D1,D2}. %which incorporates the concept of spin-$\frac{1}{2}$ and the notion of holes. 
%In terms of electrons and holes, the Dirac equation describes the coupling between these two Dirac particles. Since the energy difference between electrons and holes is on the order of MeV, one may integrate out the hole part and derive the effective Hamiltonian of electrons. In zero-field cases, this projection process leads to the spinless Schrodinger equation. However, under magnetic fields, an extra Zeeman term naturally arises with Land\'e $g$-factor of 2 \cite{Dirac1,Dirac2,Dirac3}. 

It is derived from the Dirac equation that, microscopically the Zeeman effect is induced by the orbital effect through coupling between electrons and holes \cite{D1,D2}. For clearness, in the rest of this manuscript, we refer to the Zeeman effect that derived from the fundamental Dirac equation as the \textit{intrinsic} Zeeman effect.
%Under electric fields, the spin-orbit coupling could also be derived from Dirac equation through this projection procedure. 

In recent developments of condensed matter physics, one important trend is to mimic fundamental principles in condensed matter systems (such as the Dirac equation in graphene \cite{graphene1,graphene2} and topological insulators \cite{TI0,TI1,TI2,TI3}), 
where we physicists are able to tune related parameters \cite{tune1,tune2,tune3,tune4,tune5}, and search for exotic physics, in particular in topological materials \cite{TI1,TI2,TI3,TI4,exotic1,exotic2}.

The topological surface states of a topological insulator can be described by the massless Dirac equation in two dimensions. 
Recently, moiré superlattices have been found on the surface of topological insulators due to the rotational misalignment of topmost layers, which can be modeled by the massless Dirac fermion in a moiré superlattice potential as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(a).
%To realize multiple Dirac points and introduce the Dirac point coupling, we put the topological surface state in a moiré superlattice as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(a). 
The so-called moiré surface states will be formed, as proposed and studied by T. Wang \textit{et. al} \cite{MSS1} and J. Cano \textit{et. al} \cite{MSS2} independently, where multiple Dirac points are formed due to band folding and topological protection.

In this work, we are going to show that, in moiré surface states, through the mechanism of Dirac point coupling, an extra Zeeman term besides the intrinsic one can be induced by the orbital effect of the magnetic field.
%Under the moiré superlattice potential, the Dirac dispersion of topological surface states is folded, while high-symmetry points such as the $\Gamma$ point would remain gapless Dirac points due to topological protection \cite{MSS1,MSS2}. 
%Hence, multiple Dirac points would be expected at the $\Gamma$ point, where an extra Zeeman term will be induced by the orbital effect of an external magnetic field, through the coupling between these multiple Dirac points. %The Landau levels could also be worked out. 
%where the Dirac point is gapped out, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(c).
%Under an out-of-plane magnetic field, the Dirac point with induced $g$-factor will be gapped out.
We first derive the induced Zeeman effect at the $\Gamma$ point analytically, and work out the corresponding Landau levels.
We then carry out numerical simulations of moiré surface states, where the intrinsic Zeeman effect is neglected.
For comparison, we assume the moiré superlattice is formed on the top layer but not the bottom layer of the topological insulator, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(a), and corresponding Dirac cones of two surfaces are as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(b). 
Under an out-of-plane magnetic field, top-layer surface states affected by the moiré potential will be gapped out, while the bottom-layer surface states without moiré superlattice will not, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig1}(c).
Consequently, the Landau levels without and with the induced Zeeman effect are plotted in Figs. \ref{fig1}(d) and (e), respectively.
%Following the generic analytical results, we employ tight-binding models for moiré surface states and carry out detailed numerical simulations. 
In the end, we discuss possible realizations and material candidates in experiments.

