\begin{definition}[Derivation run]
Let $w \in \calL(q)$. The derivation run $\paths(w,q)$ is defined inductively as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item[•] If $q = q_I$ then $w = \lambda$ and the only derivation run is $\paths(\lambda,q_I) = q_I$.
\item[•] If $q \neq q_I$ then let $b$ be the last symbol of $w$, write $w = w' \cdot b$ and let $q' \in \pred(q,b)$ be the first $b$-predecessor of $q$ such that $w' \in \calL(q')$. Then $\paths(w,q) = \paths(w',q') \xrightarrow{b} q$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition} 
