	In Figure \ref{fig:mu1}, we  investigate the impact of the  parameter  \(\mu_{i1}\) on the feedback strategy \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) with competition concerns and the feedback  feedback  strategy \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) without competition concerns. We generate the type vector \((x_i, b_i, \xi_i, \sigma_i, \phi_i, \gamma_i, \mu_{i2})_{i = 1}^{1000}\) by sampling 1000 times from the uniform distribution \(U([0.1,1]\times [0.1,0.2]\times [0.01,0.05]^2\times [0,1]\times [1,10]\times [0,1])\), and sample 999 times from the uniform distribution \(U([1,10])\) to obtain the sequence \(\{\mu_{j1}\}_{j = 1, j\neq i}^{1000}\). Specifically, for \(i = 500\), we set \((x_{500}, b_{500}, \xi_{500}, \sigma_{500}, \phi_{500}, \gamma_{500}, \mu_{500,2})=(0.94,0.19,0.03,0.05,0.97,7.87,0.67)\). We also set \(r=0.05\) and \(\lambda=0.5\).
Figure \ref{fig:mu1} shows both the feedback strategy \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) and  \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) increase as the parameter \(\mu_{i1}\) increases. When the parameter \(\mu_{i1}\) is higher, the \(i\)-th agent allocates more wealth to maximize expected payoffs. This result is realistic, because a higher \(\mu_{i1}\) enhances the \(i\)-th agent's responsiveness to changes in the initial wealth, thereby motivating greater resource commitment to wealth accumulation. Similar to Figure \ref{fig:gamma}, the curve of \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) considering competition lies above the curve of the optimal strategy \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) without competition. This implies that the agents also consider the market competition factors in their decision-making process, and the agents adopt a more aggressive investment strategy to increase the proportion of their own wealth to maintain their competitiveness in the market.


