	In Figure \ref{fig:combined},  we examine the influence of the parameter \(\mu_{i2}\) on the feedback strategy \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) with competition concerns and the feedback strategy \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) without competition concerns. We sample 1000 times from the uniform distribution \( U([0.1,1] \times [0.1,0.2] \times [0.01,0.05]^2 \times [0,1] \times [1,10] \times [0,1]) \) to generate the type vector \( (x_i, b_i, \xi_i, \sigma_i, \phi_i, \gamma_i, \mu_{i1})_{i = 1}^{1000} \), and sample 999 times from the uniform distribution \( U([1,10]) \) to obtain the sequence \( \{\mu_{j2}\}_{j = 1, j\neq i}^{1000} \). Specifically, for \( i = 500 \), we set \( (x_{500}, b_{500}, \xi_{500}, \sigma_{500}, \phi_{500}, \gamma_{500}, \mu_{500,1})=(0.29,0.14,0.04,0.01,0.74,9.53,0.73) \).  We also set \(r=0.05\) and \(\lambda=1\). As shown in Figure \ref{fig:combined},  both \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) and \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) increase as the parameter \(\mu_{i2}\) increases. However, the investments in the risky asset do not change significantly with variations in \(\mu_{i2}\), suggesting that the investment strategies \(\widehat{\pi}_i\) and \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) are less sensitive to this parameter compared to others.
%	When the parameter \(\mu_{i2}\) is higher, the \(i\)-th agent allocates a greater proportion of its wealth to high-return assets to maximize its wealth returns.  Furthermore,  Fig. \ref{fig:combined} also shows that the curve of \(\hat{\pi}_i\) with competition concerns  lies above  the curve of the optimal strategy \(\tilde{\pi}_i\) without competition concerns. This indicates that the agents also consider market competition factors when making decisions. In a competitive market, such strategic behavior drives agents to adopt more aggressive portfolios by employing aggressive asset allocation to generate higher returns.  This choice of investment strategy reflects  the sensitivity of agents to both intrinsic risk-reward trade-offs and competitive market pressures.
