\section{Discussions}

The findings from Case~1 and Case~2 highlight the value of multi-objective optimization in achieving reliable and efficient BESS operation. In Case~1, a fixed-priority framework was used where availability loss had the highest priority, ensuring that system availability was never compromised. This configuration provided balanced performance across inverter, battery, and derating efficiencies, although it limited the exploration of trade-offs among these competing objectives. Case~2, on the other hand, employed a Pareto analysis by varying the weights between the inverter and battery loss objectives. The results clearly demonstrated a conflict: Scenario~S1 maximized inverter efficiency but incurred higher battery losses and reduced derating efficiency due to concentrated current flow; Scenario~S3 minimized battery losses by distributing current across strings, achieving higher battery and derating efficiencies but lower inverter efficiency. Scenario~S2 emerged as a balanced solution, offering the highest system efficiency while maintaining strong performance across all key performance indicators. 
